{"abstract":"This paper investigates engagement strategies for pocket-sized social robots designed to aid children in the longer term with anxiety disorders. We explore engagement strategies for a pocket-sized tactile robot designed to support children with anxiety through daily interaction. The robot delivers either synthetic emotional feedback or gamified point rewards to encourage user participation. We evaluated these strategies through two studies conducted in contrasting age groups and settings: a school-based preference study with young children, and a full-day behavioral study with university students in naturalistic environments. The study with school children (aged 6-8, n=16) indicated a preference for emotional engagement over points-based approaches. The follow up study with university students (n=14) across a full day of interactions revealed contrasting results: points-based systems produced significantly higher task accuracy (p < 0.05) and sustained performance over time. Points-based participants also rated their robot as more human-like and likable, challenging assumptions about emotional expressiveness being necessary for positive robot perception. These findings across different age groups suggest that stated preferences may not predict behavioral effectiveness, with structured reward mechanisms demonstrating superior performance outcomes despite lower user preference ratings in younger populations. This work contributes insights into age-related differences in engagement strategy effectiveness in human-robot interaction design, highlighting the importance of behavioral validation alongside preference assessment in therapeutic robotics.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828167"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Anxiety disorder","Human robot interaction","Social robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"synthetic emotions and gamification exploring engagement strategies for small social robots in different age groups","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-828167","ids":["itu-9166568c-a8d0-447f-8e1b-106eacb24fa0"],"keyword":["Anxiety disorder","Engagement strategies","Human-robot interaction","Social robots","Synthetic emotions,"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-115A4F948EB45D15F4D6BD73B8D79083"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9166568c-a8d0-447f-8e1b-106eacb24fa0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Synthetic emotions and Gamification: Exploring Engagement Strategies for Small Social Robots in different age-groups","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828167","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Lauridsen, Kasper Munch","Støy, Julie","Winther-Larsen, Anne","Abildgaard, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-709464"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Abildgaard, Anders"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"deceived by elevated a000000000001c cases of misdiagnosed diabetes","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85183561471","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-709464","identifier":[{"id":"85183561471","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38230327","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10788644","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-dae6fb16-6ea4-4c56-8de3-c520b81face2"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C1B1F399821B584C0B20C639BF242822"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - SDCA-Steno Diabetes Center Aarhus"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kasper Munch","key":"lauridsenpau","last":"Lauridsen","locid":"fee19808-60f1-4480-8d71-da0d0c2a1dc3","name":"Lauridsen, Kasper Munch","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Julie","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"28747cdd-90b5-4aeb-ad1c-452dc93b49c0","name":"Støy, Julie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anne Winther","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"162faab4-e6ad-4fce-8149-eafe733901ec","name":"Winther-Larsen, Anne","name_org":"Larsen, Anne Winther","orcid":"0000-0002-2763-595X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"abildgaardpau","last":"Abildgaard","locid":"61216b63-4285-4336-9411-544d91a127e6","name":"Abildgaard, Anders","orcid":"0000-0001-7272-0953","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38230327"],"pub":{"doi":"10.2337/cd23-0043","issn":["08918929","19454953"],"issn_org":["08918929"],"issue":"1","journal":"Clinical Diabetes","pages":"150-155","publisher":"American Diabetes Association Inc.","status":"p","title":"Clinical Diabetes","title_org":"Clinical Diabetes (Alexandria)","volume":"42"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85183561471"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-dae6fb16-6ea4-4c56-8de3-c520b81face2"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Deceived by Elevated A1C: Cases of Misdiagnosed Diabetes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-709464","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"A common denominator for most therapy treat- ments for children who suffer from an anxiety disorder is daily practice routines to learn techniques needed to overcome anxiety. However, using the techniques while experiencing anxiety can be highly challenging. This paper presents the design, implementation, and pilot study of a tactile hand-held therapeutic pocket robot “AffectaPocket”, designed to work alongside therapy as a focus object to facilitate coping during an anxiety attack. The robot does not require daily practice to be used, has a small form factor, and has been designed for children 7 to 12 years old. The pocket robot works by sensing when it is being held during anxiety and attempts to shift the child’s focus by presenting them with a simple three-note rhythm-matching game. We conducted a pilot study of the pocket robot involving four healthy children aged 7 to 11 years, and then an expanded study with 18 healthy children. Both studies aimed to assess the reliability of the robot’s sensor configuration, its design, and the effectiveness of the user tutorial. The results indicate that the morphology and sensor setup performed adequately and the tutorial process enabled the children to use the robot with little practice. This paper argues that the presented pocket robot could represent a step toward developing low-cost accessible technologies to help children suffering from anxiety disorders.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper","Mataric, Maja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798562"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Southern California"],"keyword":["Anxiety","HRI","Play","Robots","Tactile","Therapeutic"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"toward anxiety reducing therapeutic pocket robots for children","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/UR61395.2024.10597525","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798562","identifier":[{"id":"85200703809","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-de1839b5-30f5-4095-8e99-785aa93d07d8"],"keyword":["Anxiety","HRI","Play","Robots","Tactile","Therapeutic"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-A5EF78A78CF27574BCBD9463E348CAF3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/UR61395.2024.10597525","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maja","key":"mataricpau","last":"Mataric","name":"Mataric, Maja","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ur61395.2024.10597525","isbn":["9798350361070"],"place":"New York","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings from the 2024 Conference on Ubiquitous Robots."},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-85200703809"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-de1839b5-30f5-4095-8e99-785aa93d07d8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Toward anxiety-reducing therapeutic pocket robots for children","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798562","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"A common denominator for most therapy treat- ments for children who suffer from an anxiety disorder is daily practice routines to learn techniques needed to overcome anxiety. However, applying those techniques while experiencing anxiety can be highly challenging. This paper presents the design, implementation, and pilot study of a tactile hand- held pocket robot “AffectaPocket”, designed to work alongside therapy as a focus object to facilitate coping during an anxiety attack. The robot does not require daily practice to be used, has a small form factor, and has been designed for children 7 to 12 years old. The pocket robot works by sensing when it is being held and attempts to shift the child’s focus by presenting them with a simple three-note rhythm-matching game. We conducted a pilot study of the pocket robot involving four children aged 7 to 10 years, and then a main study with 18 children aged 6 to 8 years; neither study involved children with anxiety. Both studies aimed to assess the reliability of the robot’s sensor configuration, its design, and the effectiveness of the user tutorial. The results indicate that the morphology and sensor setup performed adequately and the tutorial process enabled the children to use the robot with little practice. This work demonstrates that the presented pocket robot could represent a step toward developing low-cost accessible technologies to help children suffering from anxiety disorders.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper","Mataric, Maja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808914"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Southern California"],"keyword":["Anxiety disorder","Human robot interaction","Psychology","Robot","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"toward anxiety reducing pocket robots for children","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162639/Toward_anxiety_reducing_socially_assistive_therapeutic_robots-4.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-808914","ids":["itu-63bd73e8-8270-43af-bc72-5aaeb00ddaed"],"keyword":["anxiety disorder","anxiety research","human-robot interaction","psychology","robot","robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-05226110A50F48E36A22335C3EED061D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162639/Toward_anxiety_reducing_socially_assistive_therapeutic_robots-4.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maja","key":"mataricpau","last":"Mataric","name":"Mataric, Maja","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-63bd73e8-8270-43af-bc72-5aaeb00ddaed"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Toward Anxiety-Reducing Pocket Robots for Children","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808914","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Children diagnosed with anxiety disorders are taught a range of strategies to navigate situations of heightened anxiety. Techniques such as deep breathing and repetition of mantras are commonly employed, as they are known to be calming and reduce elevated heart rates. Although these strategies are often effective, their successful application relies on prior training of the children for successful use when faced with challenging situations. This paper investigates a pocket-sized companion robot designed to offer a relaxation technique requiring no prior training, with a focus on immediate impact on the user’s heart rate. The robot utilizes a tactile game to divert the user’s attention, thereby promoting relaxation. We conducted two studies with children who were not diagnosed with anxiety: a 14-day pilot study with two children (age 8) and a main study with 18 children (ages 7-8). Both studies employed a within-subjects design and focused on measuring heart rate during tactile interaction with the robot and during non-use. Interacting with the robot was found to significantly lower the study participants’ heart rate (p<0.01) compared to the nonuse condition, indicating a consistent calming effect across all participants. These results suggest that tactile companion robots have the potential to enhance the therapeutic value of relaxation techniques.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper","Mataric, Maja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818240"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Southern California"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tactile comfort lowering heart rate through interactions with a pocket robot","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733615/Tactile_Comfort_Lowering_Heart_Rate_through_Touch_Interactions_with_a_Pocket_Robot-6.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-818240","identifier":[{"id":"85216466286","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-57c559d3-0c84-4d4a-be28-a23e4fcdbcbf"],"keyword":["robotics, anxiety, socially assistive robotics, biofeedback."],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-5808065BE8D0A8B16042F4D2BA1B7CB5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733615/Tactile_Comfort_Lowering_Heart_Rate_through_Touch_Interactions_with_a_Pocket_Robot-6.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maja","key":"mataricpau","last":"Mataric","name":"Mataric, Maja","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/iros58592.2024.10802076","status":"p","title":"Proceedings from 2024 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS). IEEE, 2024."},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85216466286"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-57c559d3-0c84-4d4a-be28-a23e4fcdbcbf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Tactile Comfort: Lowering Heart Rate Through Interactions with a Pocket Robot","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818240","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This paper presents Affecta-context, a general framework to facilitate behavior adaptation for social robots. The framework uses information about the physical context to guide its behaviors in human-robot interactions. It consists of two parts: one that represents encountered contexts and one that learns to prioritize between behaviors through human-robot interactions. As physical contexts are encountered the framework clusters them by their measured physical properties. In each context, the framework learns to prioritize between behaviors to optimize the physical attributes of the robot’s behavior in line with its current environment and the preferences of the users it interacts with. This paper illlustrates the abilities of the Affecta-context framework by enabling a robot to autonomously learn the prioritization of discrete behaviors. This was achieved by training across 72 interactions in two different physical contexts with 6 different human test participants. The paper demonstrates the trained Affecta-context framework by verifying the robot’s ability to generalize over the input and to match its behaviors to a previously unvisited physical context.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712502"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective impact","Autonomous systems","Behaviour","Contact","Learning","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"affecta context the context guided behavior adaptation framework","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733193/Affecta-Context_-_The_Context-Guided_Behavior_Adaptation_Framework.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This Project was funded by The Independent Research Fund Denmark"],"id":"S01-000.00-712502","identifier":[{"id":"85192156819","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2126202a-b8cb-405d-81f0-98c49a376eb9"],"keyword":["Affective impact","Autonomous systems","Behaviour","Contact","Robotics","learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AB749BB7E3BD8B7900940F9BC903D3EE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109733193/Affecta-Context_-_The_Context-Guided_Behavior_Adaptation_Framework.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-44981-9_1","eisbn":["9783031449819"],"isbn":["9783031449802"],"pages":"3-15","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Intelligent Autonomous Systems 18 (IAS 2023)","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85192156819"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2126202a-b8cb-405d-81f0-98c49a376eb9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Affecta-Context: The Context-Guided Behavior Adaptation Framework","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712502","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"To improve user personalization of robots in social situations, robots can benefit from inferring information about the humans with whom they interact. Physical human behaviors and personality traits have previously been touted as possible sources of such information but often require complex processing or sensoring requirements. This paper investigates posing specific questions related to extrovert behaviors as an alternative source of this information. It aims to highlight significant relationships between questions aimed at behavioral reactions in specific scenarios and speech and movement attributes, obtained by a robot in a one-on-one social interaction. The paper used an experiment in which participants interacted with a robot through a non-scripted conversation. In it, the robot would gather information on the speech and movement characteristics, and estimated arousal/valence levels of the participant. The experiment was followed by a series of specific questions aimed at outlining the extroversion level of the participants. The results showed multiple significant but weak correlations (p<.05) between the recorded attributes. These include correlations between the average determined valence and the average recorded velocity of speech, between the average answer reaction time and average answer length. The results also found correlations between arousal levels, average pause duration, and the answers recorded for individual questions of the questionnaire. The results suggest that introducing specific questions in human-robot interactions can potentially be used to decrease the processing and sensor demands of robots and offer user personalization using only a limited set of sensors.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818246"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Human robot interaction","Personalization","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the art of inquiry toward robots that infer speech and movement characteristics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85184814105","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818246","identifier":[{"id":"85184814105","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-842f2b19-a7d2-4cb8-9784-6dcf9be5c4da"],"keyword":["Human-Robot Interaction","Open-ended interaction","Personalization","Robot inquiry","Robotics","Speech and movement characteristics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0E3E330D28A52C25F5BDAEDA03AFC249"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9798350327458"],"isbn":["9798350327465"],"pages":"1-8","place":"Cambridge, MA, USA","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"11th International Conference on Affective Computing and Intelligent Interaction Workshops and Demos (ACIIW)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85184814105"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-842f2b19-a7d2-4cb8-9784-6dcf9be5c4da"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Art of Inquiry: Toward Robots that Infer Speech and Movement Characteristics","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818246","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"This work presents a novel sensor setup for the general detection of liquid-liquid interfaces in different mixes of liquids as part of a liquid-liquid extraction device. The sensor setup is applied to a laboratory scale separatory funnel. It uses a near infrared sensor array which receives light going through the liquids inside the funnel, which are illuminated by a light source located on the other side and below the funnel. Light refracts inside the funnel and the liquids and reflects on the interface creating changing patterns in the light intensity measured by the sensor, providing a way of locating the liquid-liquid interface. Liquid mixes with different optical features, from transparent to opaque, emulsion and clean, are used to test whether different types of interfaces produce a distinguishable response on the sensor, allowing to detect interfaces in different situations that can occur as part of an Artificial Intelligence orchestrated battery chemical synthesis process. Emulsion interfaces create a discernible change in the sensor input by lowering the light intensity registered when crossing in front of the sensor making them easier to locate than with other optical techniques. The setup opens the possibility of detecting a liquid-liquid interface as it is forming and can be miniaturized to be attached to laboratory funnels as a manual aid or used with other transparent vessels in automatic solutions like liquid handling robots or pipetting robots.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381821"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Emulsion","Liquid Liquid Extraction","NIR sensor","general LLE","liquid liquid interface"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"near infrared sensor setup for general interface detection in automatic liquid liquid extraction processes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSEN.2022.3164188","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381821","identifier":[{"id":"85127488530","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-24f06edd-5cc5-44e1-87c9-d1b89216b13e"],"keyword":["NIR sensor","emulsion","general LLE","liquid-liquid extraction","liquid-liquid interface"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-603303DE8495ED8ADD8B6D2D27C99323"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSEN.2022.3164188","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?tp=&arnumber=9745914","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101333729/Near_Infrared_Sensor_Setup_for_General_Interface_Detection_in_Automatic_Liquid-Liquid_Extraction_Processes.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/jsen.2022.3164188","issn":["1530437X","15581748"],"issn_org":["15581748"],"issue":"10","journal":"IEEE Sensors Journal","pages":"9857-9867","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"IEEE Sensors Journal","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85127488530"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-24f06edd-5cc5-44e1-87c9-d1b89216b13e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Near Infrared Sensor Setup for General Interface Detection in Automatic Liquid-Liquid Extraction Processes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381821","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"A key challenge in evolutionary robotics is the computational cost of evolutionary runs. The high computational cost forces researchers to rely on power-hungry computer clusters and, even with these, researchers often are faced with long evaluation cycles that make development of evolutionary experiments a time consuming and tedious effort. In this paper we address this challenge on two fronts. We have developed an evolutionary robotic engine where all individuals are evaluated in parallel using a thread-based implementation on a graphical processing unit (GPU). This engine allows us to run an evolutionary robotics experiment in seconds on a modest laptop. The second avenue of exploration is that we have used this engine to study the role of initial robot poses in fitness evaluation. We find that if we co-evolve initial pose and controller competitively, we can reduce the evaluation period of individuals significantly. Combined the evolutionary robotics engine and the co-evolutionary approach are significant demonstrations of how to make evolutionary robotics more computationally efficient.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383257"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Co evolution","Computational cost","Evolutionary Robotics","Fitness Evaluation","GPU Parallelism"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"efficiency through gpu based co evolution of control and pose in evolutionary robotics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00558","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383257","identifier":[{"id":"86000253956","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-87bd1f6f-c9dc-4588-88dd-fb065cf581bd"],"keyword":["Co-Evolution","Computational Cost","Evolutionary Robotics","Fitness Evaluation","GPU Parallelism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-BB1A525865021A0F192B2EB006C4E88A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00558","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102674593/isal_a_00558.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://direct.mit.edu/isal/proceedings-pdf/isal2022/34/2/2035457/isal_a_00558.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1162/isal_a_00558","issn":["26931508"],"journal":"Artificial Life Conference Proceedings","paperid":"isal_a_00558, 2","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","title":"Artificial Life Conference Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-86000253956"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-87bd1f6f-c9dc-4588-88dd-fb065cf581bd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Efficiency Through GPU-based Co-Evolution of Control and Pose in Evolutionary Robotics","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383257","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Materials with the ability to self-classify their own shape have the potential to advance a wide range of engineering applications and industries. Biological systems possess the ability not only to self-reconfigure but also to self-classify themselves to determine a general shape and function. Previous work into modular robotics systems has only enabled self-recognition and self-reconfiguration into a specific target shape, missing the inherent robustness present in nature to self-classify. In this paper we therefore take advantage of recent advances in deep learning and neural cellular automata, and present a simple modular 2D robotic system that can infer its own class of shape through the local communication of its components. Furthermore, we show that our system can be successfully transferred to hardware which thus opens opportunities for future self-classifying machines. Code available at https://github.com/kattwalker/projectcube. Video available at https://youtu.be/0TCOkE4keyc.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Walker, Kathryn Elizabeth","Berg Palm, Rasmus","Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381412"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biomimetics","Deep learning","Modular robotics","Neural Cellular Automata","self classifying systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"physical neural cellular automata for 000000000002d shape classification","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90884499/project_cube_for_IROS_2022_5.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381412","ids":["itu-0a957b67-d70e-453c-8f8b-bd5c0e3311e6"],"keyword":["biomimetics","deep learning","modular robotics","neural cellular automata","self-classifying systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-078E5851A4A04C7A48C21E04D5594FE1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90884499/project_cube_for_IROS_2022_5.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathryn Elizabeth","key":"walkerpau","last":"Walker","name":"Walker, Kathryn Elizabeth","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"bergpalmpau","last":"Berg Palm","name":"Berg Palm, Rasmus","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo Moreno","key":"garciapau","last":"Garcia","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","name_org":"Garcia, Rodrigo Moreno","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"journal":"IROS","status":"p","title":"IROS","title_org":"IROS 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0a957b67-d70e-453c-8f8b-bd5c0e3311e6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Physical Neural Cellular Automata for 2D Shape Classification","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381412","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The evaluation of inoculation processes of microorganisms by robotic systems as well as by lab-technicians is compensable and can be missing consistency as human judgment will depend on the individual and may therefore be biased and less effective than models and algorithms evaluating spatial patterns. To address this problem, nearest neighbor analysis was used to investigate if it could be utilized as a method to evaluate isolation processes. The nearest neighbor analysis results in a comparable numeric value on the isolation process, which can be used to assess results of different inoculation processes. In this article, images of Petri dishes and simulated plates are used to investigate the effectiveness of nearest neighbor analysis, which is a method within spatial statistics. This analysis is applied to spatial data created by applying computer vision to localize the colonies on the plates. When evaluating plates made with the streaking technique method, it was found to be ineffective as the dense parts of the distribution resulted in the computer vision being unable to locate all of the colonies. Therefore, the nearest neighbor analysis is not suitable to evaluate streaking plates and other methods to evaluate such plates should be developed. However, when evaluating Petri dishes where the spread plating technique had been applied, it was found that nearest neighbor analysis can be a useful way to systematically evaluate isolation processes.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Jensen, Jonas Haugaard","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382172"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Automatization","Image processing","Inoculation","Microbiology","Nearest neighbor analysis","Spatial statistics","Spread plating","Streaking"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"systematic evaluation of isolation processes of microorganisms using spatial statistics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.slast.2022.09.001","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382172","identifier":[{"id":"85143916712","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3d1a4de3-811a-4f79-88be-b1c6d1f7f7ba"],"keyword":["Automatization","Image processing","Inoculation","Microbiology","Nearest neighbor analysis","Spatial statistics","Spread plating","Streaking"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-43FCBCC07477848B92F322DA1305FBA4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.slast.2022.09.001","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Haugaard","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"05788d6a-1042-4bf1-8ed7-b11224fb483e","name":"Jensen, Jonas Haugaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.slast.2022.09.001","issn":["24726311"],"journal":"SLAS TECHNOLOGY","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"SLAS TECHNOLOGY"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85143916712"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3d1a4de3-811a-4f79-88be-b1c6d1f7f7ba"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Systematic evaluation of isolation processes of microorganisms using spatial statistics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382172","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Acoustic indices are valuable tools for measuring and tracking changes in biodiversity. However, the method used to collect acoustic index data can be made more effective by recent developments in electronics. The current process requires recording high-quality audio in the field and computing acoustic indices in the lab. This produces vast quantities of raw audio data, which limits the time that sensors can spend in the field and complicates data processing and analysis. Additionally, most field audio recorders are unable to log the full range of contextual environmental data that would help explain short-term variations. In this paper, we present the BioAcoustic Index Tool, a smart acoustic index and environmental sensor. The BioAcoustic Index Tool computes acoustic indices as audio is captured, storing only the index information, and logs temperature, humidity, and light levels. The sensor was able to operate completely autonomously for the entire five-month duration of the field study. In that time, it recorded over 4000 measurements of acoustic complexity and diversity all while producing the same amount of data that would be used to record 3 minutes of raw audio. These factors make the BioAcoustic Index Tool well-suited for large-scale, long-term acoustic biodiversity monitoring.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Kadish, David","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383604"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biodiversity","Open source","Sensor","Soundscape Ecology","acoustic index","field recorder"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bioacoustic tool long term biodiversity monitoring using on sensor acoustic index calculations","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86414768/bait.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383604","identifier":[{"id":"85112507231","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-a07ad16c-0e45-4cd3-8072-087fbfb01c93"],"keyword":["acoustic index","biodiversity","field recorder","open source","sensor","soundscape ecology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AEBB34936813FE1DFDF327A695EC563B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86414768/bait.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"David","key":"kadishpau","last":"Kadish","name":"Kadish, David","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/09524622.2021.1939786","issn":["09524622","21650586"],"issn_org":["09524622"],"journal":"Bioacoustics - the International Journal of Animal Sound and its Recording","pages":"1","publisher":"Taylor & Francis","status":"p","title":"Bioacoustics - the International Journal of Animal Sound and its Recording"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85112507231"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a07ad16c-0e45-4cd3-8072-087fbfb01c93"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"BioAcoustic Tool: long-term biodiversity monitoring using on-sensor acoustic index calculations","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383604","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"A key challenge in soft robotics is how to design self-supported large-scale soft robots. In order to address this we have created a braided manipulator. The manipulator consists of a biaxially braided cylinder made from twelve glass-fiber enforced rods. The manipulator measures 1.38m in height in its equilibrium state and has a diameter of 8.5cm at the top and 20.0cm at the base and only weighs 35g. The manipulator is fixed to a base which weighs 1.9kg in which three stepper motor driven winches are embedded at 120° intervals. From each winch a string is braided vertically through the braided manipulator and is fixed to the top. In experiments we find that the manipulator can be compressed to 51cm corresponding to 38%. The manipulator compresses in 54s which is determined by stepper speed and winch diameter and expands when the actuators are turned off in 105ms. We also find that the force required to compress the manipulator is constant at 2.6N for most of actuation range. The force generated by the manipulator corresponds to a payload of 265g which is an order of magnitude more then the weight of the manipulator itself. The limitations of the current work is that the actuation, processing, and power is externalized to the manipulator and modelling and control are unaddressed. However, overall we find that the braided manipulator is evidence that braiding holds potential as a construction paradigm for soft robotics as we have demonstrated that it allows for large-scale and light-weight soft robots.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Støy, Kasper","Walker, Kathryn Elizabeth","Nielsen, Stig Anton","Ayres, Phil","Heinrich, Mary Katherine","Leon, David A.","Cheheltan, Ashkan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381311"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Actuation Mechanism","Biaxial Braiding","Braided Manipulator","Soft Robotics","Structural design"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-U"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a large scale light weight and soft braided robot manipulator with rapid expansion capabilities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86422506/robosoft2021_rdy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381311","identifier":[{"id":"85114202292","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-03af8806-f22b-4119-bf94-7358b4aecd82"],"keyword":["Actuation Mechanism","Biaxial Braiding","Braided Manipulator","Soft Robotics","Structural Design"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-77879D753ED771FADBA3E363E516EEBC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86422506/robosoft2021_rdy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-RDKA","l3nn":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","namevar":["Royal Danish Academy"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-RDKA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathryn Elizabeth","key":"walkerpau","last":"Walker","name":"Walker, Kathryn Elizabeth","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stig Anton","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Stig Anton","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Phil","key":"ayrespau","last":"Ayres","name":"Ayres, Phil","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mary Katherine","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Mary Katherine","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"David A.","key":"leonpau","last":"Leon","name":"Leon, David A.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ashkan","key":"cheheltanpau","last":"Cheheltan","name":"Cheheltan, Ashkan","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/robosoft51838.2021.9479230","pages":"495-500","publisher":"IEEE","series":"Proceeedings, 2021 IEEE 4th International Conference on Soft Robotics","status":"p","title":"2021 IEEE 4th International Conference on Soft Robotics (RoboSoft)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85114202292"],"search":{"DAF":["All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-03af8806-f22b-4119-bf94-7358b4aecd82"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A large-scale, light-weight, and soft braided robot manipulator with rapid expansion capabilities","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381311","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Liquid-liquid extraction is a well-known and widely used process in chemistry and laboratory settings. The manual version of this process relies on visual inspection of the mixture, looking for interface formation and stabilization, and for draining the liquids separately. Different automatic devices have been developed for the purpose of separating immiscible liquids, however these solutions are often designed with a specific chemical process in mind. An automatically adjustable LLE device, that can detect and adapt to different substances can shorten adjustment time and enable automatic experimentation in laboratory settings. These kinds of devices are a key part of AI orchestrated, automatic experimentation and screening systems. To enable adaptation in a robust way, an interface detection setup that is able to detect a range of interfaces is presented in this work. The use of a light sensor, that encompasses an array of photo detectors for different light wavelengths, and an arrangement that takes advantage of the optical properties of liquids is investigated for detecting different interfaces with varying optical features. Light sensors have the advantage of not coming in contact with the chemicals involved, as they can be located outside glass vessels. An array of simple sensors reduces the complexity of the signals to be analyzed while at the same time providing enough relevant information to feed more elaborated detection techniques, i.e., machine learning approaches. A dataset is created from sensor measures, characterized, and related to different interface features. Data is automatically generated using a normal glass separatory funnel, connected to a peristaltic pump for controlling the level of liquid inside, but the setup can be adapted for different kinds of vessels and existing automation tools. An analysis of light refraction and reflection inside the funnel is performed. The light source is located on the opposite side to the light sensor and the whole setup is shielded from external light. The dataset obtained will allow the creation of machine learning models (e.g. neural networks) to improve the detection capabilities of the sensor and generalize it to not seen before interfaces. This project has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under grant agreement No 957189","author_count":3,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381933"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Lab automation","Liquid Liquid Extraction","Photoelectric sensor"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"photoelectric sensor setup and characterization for general interface detection in laboratory scale liquid liquid extraction processes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86275868/Poster.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-381933","ids":["itu-2d4aa607-ca39-4b12-aa4d-e07fb243517d"],"keyword":["Lab automation","Liquid-liquid extraction","Photoelectric sensor"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6043078A5697BD054670E83971A02C57"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86275868/Poster.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo Moreno","key":"garciapau","last":"Garcia","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","name_org":"Garcia, Rodrigo Moreno","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2d4aa607-ca39-4b12-aa4d-e07fb243517d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Photoelectric sensor setup and characterization for general interface detection in laboratory scale liquid-liquid extraction processes","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381933","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"A key challenge in soft robotics is how to design self-supported large-scale soft robots. In order to address this we have created a braided manipulator. The manipulator consists of a biaxially braided cylinder made from twelve glass-fiber enforced rods. The manipulator measures 1.38m in height in its equilibrium state and has a diameter of 8.5cm at the top and 20.0cm at the base and only weighs 35g. The manipulator is fixed to a base which weighs 1.9kg in which three stepper motor driven winches are embedded at 120◦ intervals. From each winch a string is braided vertically through the braided manipulator and is fixed to the top. In experiments we find that the manipulator can be compressed to 51cm corresponding to 38%. The manipulator compresses in 54s which is determined by stepper speed and winch diameter and expands when the actuators are turned off in 105ms. We also find that the force required to compress the manipulator is constant at 2.6N for most of actuation range. The force generated by the manipulator corresponds to a payload of 265g which is an order of magnitude more then the weight of the manipulator itself. The limitations of the current work is that the actuation, processing, and power is externalized to the manipulator and modelling and control are unaddressed. However, overall we find that the braided manipulator is evidence that braiding holds potential as a construction paradigm for soft robotics as we have demonstrated that it allows for large-scale and light-weight soft robots.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Stoy, Kasper","Walker, Karen","Nielsen, Stig Anton","Ayres, Phil","Heinrich, Mary Katherine","Leon, David Andres","Cheheltan, Ashkan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-127175"],"event":{"end":"2021-04-16","place":"New Haven, us","start":"2021-04-12","title":"IEEE International Conference on Soft Robotics","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Conferences","Force","Process control","Production","Soft Robotics","Weight measurement"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-U"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a large scale light weight and soft braided robot manipulator with rapid expansion capabilities","src":["arkc"],"src-code":["DI-ART-RDKA"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-127175","identifier":[{"id":"85114202292","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["arkc-73591653-797c-4354-851e-0966f7c6eea3"],"keyword":["Conferences","Force","No","Process control","Production","Soft robotics","Weight measurement","Winches"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4A5D758920EC1237C133C85110FC387C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-RDKA","l3nn":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","namevar":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Institute of Architecture and Technology","Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Institute of Architecture and Technology, CITA - Centre for Information Technology and Architecture"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-RDKA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"stoypau","last":"Stoy","name":"Stoy, Kasper","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Karen","key":"walkerpau","last":"Walker","name":"Walker, Karen","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stig Anton","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Stig Anton","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Phil","key":"ayrespau","last":"Ayres","locid":"809e3672-ddc8-44e7-8a1b-1c1309974305","name":"Ayres, Phil","orcid":"0000-0002-9266-065X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mary Katherine","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"08c85002-6341-40a3-9046-b6222a08f965","name":"Heinrich, Mary Katherine","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"David Andres","key":"leonpau","last":"Leon","name":"Leon, David Andres","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ashkan","key":"cheheltanpau","last":"Cheheltan","name":"Cheheltan, Ashkan","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["arkc-73591653-797c-4354-851e-0966f7c6eea3"],"src":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation"],"title":"A large-scale, light-weight, and soft braided robot manipulator with rapid expansion capabilities","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-127175","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In evolutionary robotics, we evaluate individuals by placing them in an initial configuration in the environment, and then measure their fitness over a period of time. The choice of initial configuration has a direct impact on the fitness of an individual and thereby also the overarching evolutionary process. In this paper, we propose the concept of dynamic initial configurations, which is an initial configuration that is neither random nor fixed, but develops dynamically in response to the evolutionary process. As an example we have implemented a competitive co-evolutionary algorithm where initial configurations and controllers are evolved together to solve an obstacle avoidance task of a mobile robot. We show that, while a evolutionary approach taken from literature consistently fails, the co-evolutionary approach succeeds in 22 out of 25runs. This example demonstrates the benefit of dynamic initial configurations, but more work is needed to establish if the concept generalizes to more complex tasks, environments and morphologies.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382175"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Co evolutionary Algorithms","Dynamic Initial Configurations","Evolutionary Robotics","Fitness Evaluation","Obstacle avoidance"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"co evolution of initial configuration and control in evolutionary robotics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86385531/ALIFE2021_Co_evolution.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382175","identifier":[{"id":"86000220369","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3d361ed4-dd54-4018-bbe8-65feb8f07195"],"keyword":["Co-evolutionary Algorithms","Dynamic Initial Configurations","Evolutionary Robotics","Fitness Evaluation","Obstacle Avoidance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-43CA45358E64076D705BFD123669FFF9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86385531/ALIFE2021_Co_evolution.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["26931508"],"journal":"Artificial Life Conference Proceedings","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","title":"Artificial Life Conference Proceedings","title_org":"ALIFE : proceedings of the artificial life conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-86000220369"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3d361ed4-dd54-4018-bbe8-65feb8f07195"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Co-evolution of Initial Configuration and Control in Evolutionary Robotics","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382175","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Speech characteristics have shown potential as a tool to identify personality factors in humans but usually demands longer interactions or elaborate sensor requirements. This paper presents a novel robot system that uses speech and body movement characteristics to recognize and distinguish between users-groups interacting with it through small sets of interactions. It clusters people with similar characteristics together and measures the affective impact of specific robot behaviors. The system was tested using a custom-created affec- tive robot through 36 interactions with 6 human participants aging from 11 to 70. 186 samples were collected in two different physical contexts and the similarity of the samples for each user was compared. The preliminary results indicate that the speech and movement characteristics have the potential as a tool to recognize specific users and as a guide to form user groups. This was found using only basic sensors available in most robots through a limited set of interactions. The results further highlight that there are significant differences between measurements for the same users in different physical contexts meaning that the participants move and talk differently with each context. This paper suggests combining the speech and movement characteristics with information on the physical context to gain better user adaptation in robot behaviors for future projects.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808909"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Atmospheric measurements","Particle measurements","Robot sensing systems","Speech analysis","Speech coding","Time measurement","Tools"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a minimalistic approach to user group adaptation of robot behaviors using movement and speech analysis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/RO-MAN50785.2021.9515540","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-808909","identifier":[{"id":"85115076472","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-296fffc1-0902-479b-a436-29e42579893e"],"keyword":["Atmospheric measurements","Particle measurements","Robot sensing systems","Speech analysis","Speech coding","Time measurement","Tools"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-246ADDFCE3D46B8FBF916A35DD87B830"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109619244/A_minimalistic_approach_to_user_group_adaption.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/RO-MAN50785.2021.9515540","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution and Artificial Life Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ro-man50785.2021.9515540","eisbn":["9781665404921"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2021 30th IEEE International Conference on Robot &amp; Human Interactive Communication (RO-MAN)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115076472"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-296fffc1-0902-479b-a436-29e42579893e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Minimalistic Approach to User Group Adaptation of Robot Behaviors using Movement and Speech Analysis","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808909","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In an effort to improve how robots function in social contexts, this paper investigates if a robot that actively shares a reaction to an event with a human alters how the human perceives the robot's affective impact. To verify this, we created two different test setups. One to highlight and isolate the reaction element of affective robot expressions, and one to investigate the effects of applying specific timing delays to a robot reacting to a physical encounter with a human. The first test was conducted with two different groups (n=84) of human observers, a test group and a control group both interacting with the robot. The second test was performed with 110 participants using increasingly longer reaction delays for the robot with every ten participants. The results show a statistically significant change (p<; .05) in perceived affective impact for the robots when they react to an event shared with a human observer rather than reacting at random. The result also shows for shared physical interaction, the near-human reaction times from the robot are most appropriate for the scenario. The paper concludes that a delay time around 200ms may render the biggest impact on human observers for small-sized non-humanoid robots. It further concludes that a slightly shorter reaction time around 100ms is most effective when the goal is to make the human observers feel they made the biggest impact on the robot.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382833"],"event":{"acronym":"RO-MAN","title":"IEEE International Symposium on Robot and Human Interactive Communication"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective impact","Human robot interaction","Physical Encounter","Reaction Timing","Social robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the causality between affective impact and coordinated human robot reactions","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694758/On_the_causality_between_affective_impact_and_coordinated_human_robot_reactions.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382833","identifier":[{"id":"85095760386","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-68b89e6a-b966-4696-afa6-162211e5479c"],"keyword":["Affective Impact","Human-Robot Interaction","Physical Encounter","Reaction Timing","Social Robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BBDBCBC6F95B12631539973B11D9DEE5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694758/On_the_causality_between_affective_impact_and_coordinated_human_robot_reactions.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ro-man47096.2020.9223435","eisbn":["9781728160757"],"pages":"488-494","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2020 29th IEEE International Conference on Robot and Human Interactive Communication (RO-MAN)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85095760386"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-68b89e6a-b966-4696-afa6-162211e5479c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the causality between affective impact and coordinated human-robot reactions","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382833","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Commercial liquid handling robots are rarely appropriate when tasks change often, which is the case in the early stages of biochemical research. In order to address it, we have developed EvoBot, a liquid handling robot, which is open-source and employs a modular design. The combination of an open-source and a modular design is particularly powerful because functionality is divided into modules with simple, well-defined interfaces, hence customisation of modules is possible without detailed knowledge of the entire system. Furthermore, the modular design allows end-users to only produce and assemble the modules that are relevant for their specific application. Hence, time and money are not wasted on functionality that is not needed. Finally, modules can easily be reused. In this paper, we describe the EvoBot modular design and through scientific experiments such as basic liquid handling, nurturing of microbial fuel cells, and droplet chemotaxis experiments document how functionality is increased one module at a time with a significant amount of reuse. In addition to providing wet-labs with an extendible, open-source liquid handling robot, we also think that modularity is a key concept that is likely to be useful in other robots developed for scientific purposes.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Faina, Andres","Nejati, Brian","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384598"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Toronto"],"keyword":["Droplets","Evolutionary algorithms","Liquid handling robots","Microbial fuel cells","Modular Robots","Open source robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evobot an open source modular liquid handling robot for scientific experiments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app10030814","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384598","identifier":[{"id":"85081599621","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e1f88b1b-fcd2-4e93-a144-042a7faf0927"],"keyword":["Droplets","Evolutionary Algorithms","Liquid handling robots","Microbial Fuel Cells","Modular Robots","Open-source robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F33652451957EA5363DFA86866F1D041"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app10030814","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152786/applsci_10_00814_v3.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"university of toronto","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Toronto","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Brian","key":"nejatipau","last":"Nejati","name":"Nejati, Brian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/app10030814","issn":["20763417"],"issue":"3","journal":"Applied Sciences","paperid":"814","publisher":"Balkan Society of Geometers","status":"p","title":"Applied Sciences","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85081599621"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e1f88b1b-fcd2-4e93-a144-042a7faf0927"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EvoBot: An Open-Source, Modular, Liquid Handling Robot for Scientific Experiments","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384598","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The paper introduces a fully automated cultivation and monitoring tool to study biofilm development in replicate experiments operated in parallel. To gain a fundamental understanding of the relation between cultivation conditions and biofilm characteristics (e.g., structural, mechanical) a monitoring setup allowing for the standardization of methods is required. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) is an imaging modality ideal for biofilms since it allows for the monitoring of structure in real time. By integrating an OCT device into the open-source robotic platform EvoBot, a fully automated monitoring platform for investigating biofilm development in several flow cells at once was realized. Different positioning scenarios were tested and revealed that the positioning accuracy is within the optical resolution of the OCT. On that account, a reliable and accurate monitoring of biofilm development by means of OCT has become possible. With this robotic platform, reproducible biofilm experiments including a statistical analysis are achievable with only a small investment of operator time. Furthermore, a number of structural parameters calculated within this study confirmed the necessity to perform replicate biofilm cultivations.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Gierl, Luisa","Støy, Kasper","Faina, Andres","Horn, Harald","Wagner, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384931"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karlsruhe Institute of Technology","Karlsruher Institut für Technologie"],"keyword":["Automated cultivation","Biofilm monitoring","Optical coherence tomography","Robotic platforms","Structural biofilm analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an open source robotic platform that enables automated monitoring of replicate biofilm cultivations using optical coherence tomography","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41522-020-0129-y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384931","identifier":[{"id":"85082979690","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f8bd4f54-bc25-45e3-9574-e98971f85ecc"],"keyword":["Automated cultivation","Biofilm monitoring","Optical coherence tomography","Robotic platforms","Structural biofilm analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6BA96D798E8B2ACDE0B320BE799DF6F7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152853/s41522_020_0129_y.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41522-020-0129-y","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"karlsruhe institute of technology","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Karlsruhe Institute of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"karlsruher institut für technologie","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Karlsruher Institut für Technologie","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luisa","key":"gierlpau","last":"Gierl","name":"Gierl, Luisa","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Harald","key":"hornpau","last":"Horn","name":"Horn, Harald","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"wagnerpau","last":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41522-020-0129-y","issn":["20555008"],"journal":"npj Biofilms and Microbiomes","paperid":"18","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"npj Biofilms and Microbiomes","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85082979690"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f8bd4f54-bc25-45e3-9574-e98971f85ecc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An open-source robotic platform that enables automated monitoring of replicate biofilm cultivations using optical coherence tomography","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384931","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Abstract—This paper investigates the specific scenario of high-intensity confrontations between humans and robots, to understand how robots can defuse the conflict. It focuses on the effectiveness of using five different affective expression modalities as main drivers for defusing the conflict. The aim is to discover any strengths or weaknesses in using each modality to mitigate the hostility that people feel towards a poorly performing robot. The defusing of the situation is accomplished by making the robot better at acknowledging the conflict and by letting it express remorse. To facilitate the tests, we used a custom affective robot in a simulated conflict situation with 105 test participants. The results show that all tested expression modalities can successfully be used to defuse the situation and convey an acknowledgment of the confrontation. The ratings were remarkably similar, but the movement modality was different (ANON p<.05) than the other modalities. The test participants also had similar affective interpretations on how impacted the robot was of the confrontation across all expression modalities. This indicates that defusing a high- intensity interaction may not demand special attention to the expression abilities of the robot, but rather require attention to the abilities of being socially aware of the situation and reacting in accordance with it.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383781"],"event":{"acronym":"IROS","title":"IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Robot conflict interaction behavior engineering"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"robots can defuse high intensity conflict situations","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694792/Robots_can_defuse_high_intensity_conflict_situations.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383781","identifier":[{"id":"85102397718","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ad2e6f1a-c149-40a1-aed6-855378d3e6ae"],"keyword":["Robot, conflict, interaction, behavior, engineering"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FA49183568554A8D710D6612ADB39CB7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://ras.papercept.net/images/temp/IROS/files/0200.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85694792/Robots_can_defuse_high_intensity_conflict_situations.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781728162119/20"],"pages":"11376-11382","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ad2e6f1a-c149-40a1-aed6-855378d3e6ae"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Robots can defuse high-intensity conflict situations","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383781","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"This paper provides a survey of the different means of expression employed by robots, to convey affective state to human recipients. The paper introduces a model of affective means (MOAM) to effectively describe and compare the emphasis on specific means, and applies it to the surveyed robots. The model-based survey shows that a majority (92\\%) of the surveyed robots contain a category with room for additional affective means, while a quarter (25.6\\%) of the robots use a single or two affective means of expression to convey affective states. The result of the survey indicates there is an under researched opportunity in exploring synergies between means of affective expression to amplify the overall affective impact of a robot.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381342"],"event":{"acronym":"IROS","title":"IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective","Expression","Robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a systematic comparison of affective robot expression modalities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"GNU GPL","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84786769/A_Systematic_comparison_of_affective_robot_expression_modalities_pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-381342","identifier":[{"id":"85081168097","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-057663ad-eb2e-4b29-a8a9-fce341eb829b"],"keyword":["Affective","Expression","Robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FB6E31DB0383D5028C430B1132C993F4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"GNU GPL","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84786769/A_Systematic_comparison_of_affective_robot_expression_modalities_pre_print.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781728140049"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems 2019"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85081168097"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-057663ad-eb2e-4b29-a8a9-fce341eb829b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A systematic comparison of affective robot expression modalities","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381342","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs) are bio-electrochemical devices that convert organic matter into electricity through bacterial digestion and respiration [1]. MFCs, unlike a battery, can generate electricity continuously as long as fuel is supplied. This has made MFCs an attractive alternative source of electricity for low-power robots. Robots are powered via conventional mains or battery means but this is also a bottleneck for wider deployment; it is therefore envisaged that green energy sources could be one way of powering the next generation of truly autonomous robots [2]. MFCs have previously been reported to power mobile robotic systems such as; Gastrobot, the four families of EcoBot (I, II, III and IV) and Row-bot (a floating robot). However, in order for MFCs to be implemented into the next generation of autonomous agents, their power output capability needs to be improved. This was pursued using a liquid handling robot called EvoBot [3], which interacted with MFCs using a feedback loop mechanism. The real-time electrical output of the MFCs was monitored and fed back to the robot, which was triggering the robotic liquid handling head to supply fuel, in the event of underperformance. This self-managed maintenance mechanism that prompted a feeding “on demand” was able to accelerate the maturing process of the MFCs from 30 down to 6 days. Notably, it also increased the power output by almost 40% compared to the output produced by the manually maintained MFCs on the bench (19.1 mWm-2 to 26.5 mWm-2). This experiment showed the potential of using EvoBot as a maintenance machine for optimising MFCs which can then power autonomous robots.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Theodosiou, Pavlina","Mendis, A.","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper","Greenman, John","Melhuish, Chris","Ieropoulos, Ioannis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382291"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of the West of England"],"keyword":["Autonomous robots","Bio electrochemical devices","Green energy","Liquid handling robot EvoBot","Microbial Fuel Cells MFCs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evobot in service interface and interconnection of microbial fuel cells mfcs with a liquid handling robot for optimised performance","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152955/ISMET_7_Pavlina_Abstract_iai.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382291","ids":["itu-44e74f53-1ed0-403c-88aa-f6acdae2ffff"],"keyword":["Autonomous robots","Bio-electrochemical devices","Green energy","Liquid handling robot EvoBot","Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-882D54CD261032D972999DCA136A25E9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152955/ISMET_7_Pavlina_Abstract_iai.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of the west of england","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of the West of England","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pavlina","key":"theodosioupau","last":"Theodosiou","name":"Theodosiou, Pavlina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"A.","key":"mendispau","last":"Mendis","name":"Mendis, A.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"greenmanpau","last":"Greenman","name":"Greenman, John","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Chris","key":"melhuishpau","last":"Melhuish","name":"Melhuish, Chris","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ioannis","key":"ieropoulospau","last":"Ieropoulos","name":"Ieropoulos, Ioannis","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-44e74f53-1ed0-403c-88aa-f6acdae2ffff"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EvoBot in service: interface and interconnection of Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs) with a liquid handling robot for optimised performance","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382291","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This paper investigates the potential benefits of augmenting audio-based affective means of expression to strengthen the perceived intentions of a robot. Robots are often viewed as being simple machines with limited capabilities of communication. Changing how a robot is perceived, towards a more affective interpretation of its intentions, requires careful consideration of the means of expression available to the robot. It also requires alignment between these means to ensure they work in coordination with each other to make the robot easier to understand. As an effort to strengthen the affective interpretation of a soft robotic arm robot, we altered its overall expression by changing the available audio-based expression modalities. The system mitigated the naturally occurring noise from actuators and pneumatic systems and used a custom sound that supported the movement of the robot. The robot was tested by interacting with human observers (n=78) and was perceived as being significantly more curious, happy and less angry when augmented by audio that aligned with the naturally occurred robot sounds. The results show that the audio-based expression modality of robots is a valuable communication tool to consider augmenting when designing robots that convey affective information. Index Terms—affective robot audio expression","author_count":2,"contributors":["Frederiksen, Morten Roed","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808922"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Human robot interaction"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"augmenting the audio based expression modalities of a non affective robot","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162016/Augmenting_the_audio-based_expression_modality_of_a_non-affective_robot.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-808922","ids":["itu-b386dd6f-87a0-4f87-a878-d3f80cdf097d"],"keyword":["Affective robotics","Audio-based expression","Human-robot interaction","Perceived intentions","Robotic communication modalities"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4271492802E193590143598550F8BC7B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109162016/Augmenting_the_audio-based_expression_modality_of_a_non-affective_robot.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Affective Interactions & Relations Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Morten Roed","key":"frederiksenpau","last":"Frederiksen","locid":"7016a7a0-c187-4a62-baef-0780150912d1","name":"Frederiksen, Morten Roed","orcid":"0000-0002-5646-5195","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b386dd6f-87a0-4f87-a878-d3f80cdf097d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Augmenting the Audio-Based Expression modalities of a Non-Affective robot","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808922","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Biohybrid robotics takes an engineering approach to the expansion and exploitation of biological behaviors for application to automated tasks. Here we identify the construction of living buildings and infrastructure as a high-potential application domain for biohybrid robotics, and review technological advances relevant to its future development. Construction, civil infrastructure maintenance, and building occupancy in the last decades have comprised a major portion of economic production, energy consumption, and carbon emissions. Integrating biological organisms into automated construction tasks and permanent building components therefore has high potential for impact. Live materials can provide several advantages over standard synthetic construction materials, including self-repair of damage, increase rather than degradation of structural performance over time, resilience to corrosive environments, support of biodiversity, and mitigation of urban heat islands. Here we review relevant technologies, which are currently disparate. They span robotics, self-organizing systems, artificial life, construction automation, structural engineering, architecture, bioengineering, biomaterials, and molecular and cellular biology. In these disciplines, developments relevant to biohybrid construction and living buildings are in early stages, and typically are not exchanged between disciplines. We therefore consider this review useful to the future development of biohybrid engineering for this highly interdisciplinary application.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Heinrich, Mary Katherine","von Mammen, Sebastian","Hofstadler, Daniel Nicolas","Wahby, Mostafa","Zahadat, Payam","Skrzypczak, Tomasz","Divband Soorati, Mohammad","Krela, Rafal","Kwiatkowski, Wojciech","Schmickl, Thomas","Ayres, Phil","Stoy, Kasper","Hamann, Heiko"],"cuid":["S02-091.84-666042"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Germany","Poland"],"glorg":["Adam Mickiewicz University","Adam Mickiewicz University in Poznań","All Global Affiliations","Julius-Maximilians-Universität Würzburg","University of Graz","University of Lübeck","University of Würzburg"],"keyword":["Bio hybrid","Biobot","Construction","Hybrid","Robotics","Self organisation","Self organization"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-U"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"constructing living buildings a review of relevant technologies for a novel application of biohybrid robotics","src":["arkc","itu"],"src-code":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2019.0238","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-091.84-666042","identifier":[{"id":"85070269359","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["arkc-2f61f70f-9974-4ddd-86c2-49506a657f3f","itu-acd3a16c-1836-4ff8-8447-34f1f3375642"],"keyword":["Bio-hybrid","Biobot","Construction","Hybrid","No","Robotics","Self-organisation","self-organization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-94FD87EB246EF2C97AF6D027879A90ED"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84759307/rsif.2019.0238.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334062542","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2019.0238","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Art