{"abstract":"In this paper we study fractal gene regulatory network (FGRN) controllers based on sensory information. The FGRN controllers are evolved to control a snake robot consisting of seven simulated ATRON modules. Each module contains three tilt sensors which represent the direction of gravity in the coordination system of the module. The modules are controlled locally and there is no explicit communication between them. So, they can synchronize implicitly using their sensors, and coordination of their behavior takes place through the environment. In one of our experiments, all the three tilt sensors are available for the FGRNs and a simple controller is evolved. The controller is a linear mapping of one input sensor to the output. It is only based on one sensor input and ignores the other sensors as well as the regulatory part of the network. In another experiment, the controller's input uses one of the other sensors that carries less information. In this case, the evolved controller blends sensory information with the regulatory network capabilities to come up with a proper distributed controller. © 2013 Springer-Verlag.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Zahadat, Payam","Christensen, David Johan","Katebi, Serajeddin","Stoy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-337627"],"event":{"end":"2010-11-03","place":"Lausanne, ch","start":"2010-11-01","title":"10th International Symposium on Distributed Autonomous Robotic Systems (DARS 2010)","year":"2010"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, Middle East"],"country":["Iran"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Shiraz University"],"keyword":["Controllers","Experiments","Fractals","GENES","Sensors"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sensor coupled fractal gene regulatory networks for locomotion control of a modular snake robot","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-32723-0_37","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-337627","identifier":[{"id":"84870758144","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-6eb477d7-f11d-4a34-9764-e76d539f93ba"],"keyword":["Controllers","Experiments","Fractals","Genes","Sensors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CA47A7BDA18193084324F326A3F40F88"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-32723-0_37","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Centre for Playware","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Automation and Control"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ir","key":"shiraz university","loc":["Iran","Asia, Middle East"],"name":"Shiraz University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","name":"Zahadat, Payam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"David Johan","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"a721954c-7276-4e0c-a025-82fd7496b5ab","name":"Christensen, David Johan","orcid":"0000-0002-4358-4371","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Serajeddin","key":"katebipau","last":"Katebi","name":"Katebi, Serajeddin","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"stoypau","last":"Stoy","name":"Stoy, Kasper","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-642-32723-0_37","eisbn":["9783642327230"],"isbn":["9783642327223"],"issn":["16107438"],"issue":"83","pages":"517-530","publisher":"Springer Verlag","series":"Springer Tracts in Advanced Robotics","status":"p","title":"Distributed Autonomous Robotic Systems. The 10th International Symposium"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84870758144"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-6eb477d7-f11d-4a34-9764-e76d539f93ba"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Sensor-coupled fractal gene regulatory networks for locomotion control of a modular snake robot","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-337627","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Modular robots are a kind of robots built from mechatronic modules, which can be assembled in many different ways allowing the modular robot to assume a wide range of morphologies and functions. An important question in modular robotics is to which degree modules should be heterogeneous. In this paper we introduce two contributing factors to heterogeneity namely morphological heterogeneity and sub-functional modularization. Respectively, the ideas are to create modules with significantly different morphologies and to spread sub-functionality across modules. Based on these principles we design and implement the Thor robot and evaluate it by participating in the ICRA Planetary Robotic Contingency Challenge. The Thor robot demonstrates that sub-functional modularity and morphological heterogeneity may increase the versatility of modular robots while reducing the complexity of individual modules, which in the longer term may lead to more affordable modular robots. © 2013 Springer-Verlag.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Lyder, Andreas","Støy, Kasper","Garcia, Ricardo Franco Mendoza","Larsen, Jørgen Christian","Hermansen, Palle"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-619773"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on sub modularization and morphological heterogeneity in modular robotics advances in intelligent systems and computing","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-33926-4_62","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-619773","identifier":[{"id":"84872771930","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-a2befb3a-cfbc-49af-860a-753ff04c0a54"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9711685E213D39585F30B5DC87FC9DE0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-33926-4_62","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?eid=2-s2.0-84872771930&partnerID=40&md5=157e37789fce6f4580b1158c02523e54","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"A. H.","key":"lyderandreaspau","last":"Lyder","locid":"5d42b0be-9701-40ba-a2a3-51539a56266d","name":"Lyder, Andreas","name_org":"Lyder, A. H.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"K.","key":"støykasperpau","last":"Stoy","locid":"dab546c1-7862-404d-b783-4bdb8a321d52","name":"Støy, Kasper","name_org":"Stoy, K.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"R. F. M.","key":"garciaricardofrancomendozapau","last":"Garciá","locid":"d9da2d2d-b410-4092-974d-943a2cad1d97","name":"Garcia, Ricardo Franco Mendoza","name_org":"Garciá, R. F. M.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"J. C.","key":"larsenjørgenchristianpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"7360ab40-7502-495e-9745-33ad27e0e1cd","name":"Larsen, Jørgen Christian","name_org":"Larsen, J. C.","orcid":"0000-0002-4741-8290","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"P.","key":"hermansenpallepau","last":"Hermansen","locid":"351742d9-1821-4f0b-982a-e561cc9b1d96","name":"Hermansen, Palle","name_org":"Hermansen, P.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Sukhan","key":"leesukhanpedh","last":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sukhan","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Hyungsuck","key":"chohyungsuckpedh","last":"Cho","name":"Cho, Hyungsuck","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kwang-Joon","key":"yoonkwangjoonpedh","last":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Kwang-Joon","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jangmyung","key":"leejangmyungpedh","last":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jangmyung","rank":9,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-642-33926-4_62","isbn":["9783642339257"],"pages":"649-661","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","subtitle":"Volume 1 Proceedings of the 12th International Conference IAS-12, held June 26-29, 2012, Jeju Island, Korea","title":"Intelligent Autonomous Systems 12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84872771930"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-a2befb3a-cfbc-49af-860a-753ff04c0a54"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"On sub-modularization and morphological heterogeneity in modular robotics : Advances in Intelligent Systems and Computing","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-619773","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Purpose – Using a bottom-up, model-free approach when building robots is often seen as a less scientific way, compared to a top-down model-based approach, because the results are not easily generalizable to other systems. The authors, however, hypothesize that this problem may be addressed by using solid experimental methods. The purpose of this paper is to show how well-known experimental methods from bio-mechanics are used to measure and locate weaknesses in a bottom-up, model-free implementation of a quadruped walker and come up with a better solution. Design/methodology/approach – To study the bottom-up, mode-free approach, the authors used the robotic construction kit, LocoKit. This construction kit allows researchers to construct legged robots, without having a mathematical model beforehand. The authors used no specific mathematical model to design the robot, but instead used intuition and took inspiration from biology. The results were afterwards compared with results gained from biology, to see if the robot has some of the key elements the authors were looking for. Findings – With the use of LocoKit as the experimental platform, combined with known experimental measurement methods from biology, the authors have shown how a bottom-up, model-free design approach can be used to gain specific knowledge on a robotic platform, and also how knowledge can potentially be generalized from this approach. Originality/value – This paper shows that even though a bottom-up, model-free approach was taken, the results can still be compared with results from, for example, biology, because solid experimental methods were used.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Larsen, Jørgen Christian","Støy, Kasper","Brandt, David","Grimmer, Sten","Gross, Martin"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-766341"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Darmstadt University of Technology"],"keyword":["Industrial robotics","Mechanisms","Performance management","Robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"increased performance in a bottom up designed robot by experimentally guided redesign","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84880545629","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-766341","identifier":[{"id":"84880545629","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-e498d390-dbf7-43e6-bbe0-08fa27c6285d"],"keyword":["Education robots","Industrial robotics","Mechanisms","Performance management","Robot construction","Robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E03E6CF55D1BCA52BDADEE3A24E2D70A"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"darmstadt university of technology","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Darmstadt University of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jørgen Christian","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"7360ab40-7502-495e-9745-33ad27e0e1cd","name":"Larsen, Jørgen Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-4741-8290","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"dab546c1-7862-404d-b783-4bdb8a321d52","name":"Støy, Kasper","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"David","key":"brandtpau","last":"Brandt","locid":"6adb75e6-7e68-495f-85a0-128369f2e0bf","name":"Brandt, David","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sten","key":"grimmerpau","last":"Grimmer","name":"Grimmer, Sten","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"grosspau","last":"Gross","name":"Gross, Martin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1108/01439911311309933","issn":["0143991X","17585791"],"issn_org":["0143991X"],"issue":"3","journal":"Industrial Robot","pages":"238-245","publisher":"Emerald Group Publishing","status":"p","title":"Industrial Robot","volume":"40"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84880545629"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-e498d390-dbf7-43e6-bbe0-08fa27c6285d"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Increased performance in a bottom-up designed robot by experimentally guided redesign","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-766341","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, we present a distributed reinforcement learning strategy for morphology-independent lifelong gait learning for modular robots. All modules run identical controllers that locally and independently optimize their action selection based on the robot’s velocity as a global, shared reward signal.Weevaluate the strategy experimentally mainly on simulated, but also on physical, modular robots. We find that the strategy: (i) for six of seven configurations (3–12 modules) converge in 96% of the trials to the best known action-based gaits within 15 min, on average, (ii) can be transferred to physical robots with a comparable performance, (iii) can be applied to learn simple gait control tables for both M-TRAN and ATRON robots, (iv) enables an 8-module robot to adapt to faults and changes in its morphology, and (v) can learn gaits for up to 60 module robots but a divergence effect becomes substantial from 20–30 modules. These experiments demonstrate the advantages of a distributed learning strategy for modular robots, such as simplicity in implementation, low resource requirements, morphology independence, reconfigurability, and fault tolerance.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Christensen, David Johan","Schultz, Ulrik Pagh","Stoy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S03-094.51-032618"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"keyword":["Distributed control","Fault tolerance","Locomotion","Online learning","Self reconfigurable modular robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a distributed and morphology independent strategy for adaptive locomotion in self reconfigurable modular robots","src":["dtu","itu","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.robot.2013.05.009","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S03-094.51-032618","identifier":[{"id":"84880332035","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-025aaafa-0be6-4818-a77e-cdf3388686bd","itu-16734caf-b06e-4297-abbd-5ebc06b3bc12","sdu-8f1bfd7b-cbc2-4f2e-9fc8-da07b31efb09"],"keyword":["Distributed control","Fault tolerance","Locomotion","Online learning","Self-reconfigurable modular robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-213925A8FE05A2BFD3F9B9426336769F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/56235029/Christensen-RAS2013.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.robot.2013.05.009","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Centre for Playware","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Automation and Control"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"ku university of cop